def tilingRibbonPeakTicket(readings, k):
    a=readings
    out=[]
    m=max(a,default=0)
    dp=[0]*(m+1)
    dp[0]=1
    for i in range(1,m+1): dp[i]=dp[i-1]+(dp[i-3] if i>=3 else 0)
    out=[dp[x] for x in a]
    return max(out,default=0)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int tilingRibbonPeakTicket(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int m=0;for(int x:a)m=Math.max(m,x);int[]dp=new int[m+1];dp[0]=1;for(int i=1;i<=m;i++)dp[i]=dp[i-1]+(i>=3?dp[i-3]:0);for(int x:a)out.add(dp[x]);}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

For each width x, count the ordered ways to fill a one-row ribbon using pieces of widths 1 and 3. A width of 0 has one empty arrangement. Produce the counts in input order.

Return the largest value in the derived sequence, or 0 for an empty sequence. Negative maxima must remain negative.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n + M); space O(n).

